          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is a combination code, and which conditions in this reference are coded with one?",
          "answer": "**What a combination code is:** In ICD-10-CM, a combination code is one code that holds two conditions, or a condition and its complication or cause. The classification uses it in place of separate codes. The conventions document states that where a combination code exists, it \"is used instead of the separate codes\" (coding-icd10-cm-code-structure-and-conventions). Some combination codes are followed by a second code that adds detail, such as the stage of kidney disease.\n\n**Conditions in this reference that use combination codes:**\n\n- **Type 2 diabetes (E11):** The combination code names the link between diabetes and a complication. For example, diabetic chronic kidney disease is paired with a code for kidney disease stage, and a diabetic foot ulcer is paired with a code for its site and depth (coding-type-2-diabetes-e11).\n- **Hypertension (I11, I12, I13, I15):** Once organ involvement is documented, the classification moves away from the plain hypertension code I10. Hypertensive heart disease goes to I11, with a heart failure code from I50 added. Hypertension with chronic kidney disease goes to I12, with a stage code added. Both together go to I13. Hypertension due to an identifiable cause is secondary hypertension, coded to I15 together with the cause (coding-hypertension-i10).\n\n**The governing convention:** Where the Alphabetic Index links two conditions with \"with,\" the classification presumes a relationship unless the record says the two are unrelated. A record listing type 2 diabetes and chronic kidney disease is therefore coded as diabetic kidney disease by default (coding-type-2-diabetes-e11; coding-icd10-cm-code-structure-and-conventions).\n\n**What the reference does not cover:** The retrieved passages do not say which other conditions use combination codes. They also do not confirm whether E11.9 is itself a combination code. The passage only notes that it has its own code, so I have not counted it here. Asthma and heart failure are handled differently: asthma is linked to COPD by an instruction to add a code, and heart failure codes are added to hypertensive heart disease rather than combined with it.\n\nThis is a summary of how codes are assigned. It is not a diagnosis. If you are asking about a specific record, a clinician or certified coder can apply these rules to the actual documentation.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
